  "notes": [
    "Self-contained calendar-year 2026 cycle, all 52 Sundays, January 4 - December 27. Every dated entry carries the source's own liturgical Sunday name in `liturgicalNote`, a disclosed label from the source, not computed by this app.",
    "The PDF's own text uses plain/straight apostrophes and hyphens throughout (e.g. \"St. Joseph - St. John, Lakewood\", \"St. Martin - St. Francis, Rockport\"), confirmed by inspecting the raw extracted text's character codes rather than assuming from rendered appearance; this diocese's own website (ecww.org) republishes the same content with WordPress's typographic smart-quote/en-dash substitution (curly apostrophes, en dashes) but is otherwise textually identical -- used only to cross-check the parsing below, not as the transcription source per this corpus's preference for the PDF itself.",
    "Each month's heading in the source also names a deanery/region in parentheses, e.g. 'January 2026 (Columbia)', 'February 2026 (Eastside)', 'October 2026 (Ministries & Institutions)' -- not reflected per-entry here since the schema has no field for it, but noted for context: the cycle groups congregations by deanery for most of the year, then devotes October and November to diocesan-wide ministries/institutions.",
    "Two-column PDF layout (months side by side on each of 3 pages) -- verified by rendering all 3 pages as images (150dpi) and visually confirming pdftotext -layout's date-to-subject grouping against them, and additionally cross-checked against the diocese's own website, which embeds the identical cycle as a single linear (non-columnar) block -- no interleaving found.",
    "Several weeks name a specific congregation with a slash-joined bilingual/dual name, transcribed exactly as printed and not split: 'St. Matthew's/San Mateo, Auburn', 'St. Luke's/San Lucas, Vancouver', 'St. John's/San Juan, Olympia'. Likewise 'St. Michael's South Sudanese, Auburn' and 'St. John's South Sudanese, Kent' are transcribed as printed as their own single congregation names, and 'St. Joseph - St. John, Lakewood', 'St. Martin - St. Francis, Rockport', and 'St. Hilda St. Patrick, Edmonds' are each transcribed as one combined congregation name exactly as the source joins them (the last with no punctuation between the two saints' names at all, as printed).",
    "'Saint Mark's Cathedral, Seattle' (April 19) spells out 'Saint' in full where every other entry abbreviates 'St.' -- preserved exactly as printed, not normalized.",
    "October and November are devoted to diocesan-wide ministries, institutions, and governance bodies rather than congregations; each is recorded as a `category` subject with its full text (including any place the source appends after a comma, since the `category` type has no separate `place` field) preserved verbatim in `name`, e.g. 'Huston Camp & Conference Center, Gold Bar', 'Chaplains on the Harbor, Montesano' (which recurs on both September 20 and October 25, as printed -- not a duplication error), 'Annie Wright School, Tacoma', 'Charles Wright Academy, Tacoma', 'Underhill House, Seattle', and 'St. James Family Center, Cathlamet'.",
    "Two November 1 subjects and one November 22 subject wrap across two lines in the source's own layout with no internal bullet break; each was reassembled as a single subject exactly as printed: 'Retired clergy, clergy widows/widowers, and clergy households of the diocese', 'All Commissions, Committees & Programs of the Diocese', and 'The Table: University of Washington Episcopal Campus Ministry'.",
    "The source's own liturgical-Sunday numbering skips from 'Twenty-Second Sunday after Pentecost' (October 25) to 'All Saints' Sunday' (November 1) to 'Twenty-Fourth Sunday after Pentecost' (November 8) -- 'Twenty-Third' is never printed, presumably because All Saints' Sunday displaced that Proper; transcribed exactly as the source labels each date, not corrected or renumbered."
  ],
